{"status":"real","source":"NASA PSI-100 Saffire-III flight telemetry (doi 10.60555/040q-4h36), processed by models/m4-latency/build.py","y_meaning":"Signal above its pre-ignition baseline, as a multiple of that channel's alarm margin (1.0 = alarm at baseline + 5 SD)","units":{"time":"s after the event's igniter or smoke wire switched on","values":"dimensionless","alarm_s_range":"[alarm at 3 SD, alarm at 5 SD]"},"threshold_rule":"Alarm at the first reading above the 60 s pre-ignition mean + 5 SD (3 SD reported as the low end). Not the vehicle's real alarm setpoint: the smoke channels are raw, uncalibrated counts, so published thresholds in physical units (ISS: 1 %/ft obscuration; about 10 mg/m3) cannot be applied.","resolution_note":"Radiometers are logged about once a second, but their values are sample-and-hold and only change about every 5 s, so a radiometer alarm at 7 s means the signal crossed somewhere between 2 and 7 s. The smoke channels read about every 10 s, so smoke alarm times are +/- 10 s.","baseline_note":"Each event's baseline is the 60 s just before it. Before Sample 2 and Sample 1 the cabin smoke detector still reads smoke left from the event before, which raises its baseline and spread; that is why the smoke alarm times move so much with the threshold (Sample 2: 27 s at 3 SD, 127 s at 5 SD).","placement_note":"The radiometers look at the sample inside the flow duct; the smoke detector is in the Cygnus cabin. The comparison therefore mixes sensor type with sensor position.","observation":"The obscuration channel never rose. During Sample 1 it fell by at most 0.02 (about 0.5 %), below the 5 SD margin; read as falling light (more obscuration), that would cross a 3 SD margin. The pass rules, written first, count only rises, so it is reported as no alarm.","assumption":"Igniter 1 is taken as Sample 1 (concurrent) and igniter 2 as Sample 2 (opposed), following the order of the PSI-100 sample table.","burns":[{"name":"Smoke wire: an 11 s puff of smoke, no 